> 
> ---
> 
> Fragkos et al., two thousand fifteen, describe the PROMETHEUS model, which integrates both top-down and bottom-up features. PROMETHEUS combines econometrically estimated demand equations based on income and price elasticities with detailed representations of energy supply technologies across sectors and fuels, according to E3 Modelling, two thousand eighteen. This model addresses energy demand by sector and subsector, including industry, transport, and buildings. It captures the interaction between demand and supply to project energy prices endogenously at regional and global scales. PROMETHEUS features a modular structure that covers macroeconomic factors, final energy demand, electricity generation, fossil fuel markets, technology learning, and climate policy. Currently, the model includes twenty-six competing technologies for power generation. Energy prices are formed endogenously based on supply-demand balances and fossil resource constraints, while the model also simulates learning by doing and research-driven technological progress. PROMETHEUS provides a consistent framework to assess climate and energy policy impacts, fossil fuel markets, carbon dioxide emissions, as described by van der Zwaan et al., two thousand twenty-five, and energy technology evolution across ten world regions.
> 
> The Feedback Repository for Integrated Assessments, or FRIDA, is a newly developed global-scale integrated assessment model, as noted by Schoenberg et al., two thousand twenty-five. FRIDA integrates climate and human systems through feedback processes and represents the carbon, energy, and water cycles alongside changes in human demographics, economics, agriculture, and energy use. Built using the System Dynamics method—originally developed by Forrester in nineteen sixty-one and Sterman in two thousand—FRIDA represents the feedback between major components of the world-Earth system, as discussed by Donges et al., two thousand twenty-one, while maintaining a very fast runtime for uncertainty exploration. FRIDA aims to represent each part of the world-Earth system with an equivalent level of complexity to better study problems that cross traditional system or field boundaries. This approach, however, comes at the expense of detail at the process level and lacks spatial disaggregation. FRIDA natively incorporates climate impacts endogenously, with its climate module based on the widely-used FaIR simple climate model, as detailed by Smith et al., two thousand eighteen, and Leach et al., two thousand twenty-one.
> 
> The FRIDA model is validated from both behavioral and structural perspectives. Behaviorally, it is calibrated to reproduce behaviors from nineteen eighty to two thousand twenty-three across one hundred fifty-eight different time series of variables that span the full conceptual scope of the model. Structurally, validation follows the process outlined by Barlas in nineteen ninety-six, which includes ensuring dimensional consistency, proper extreme conditions behavior, and aligning the model's processes with subject matter experts and the literature’s conceptualizations of real-world processes. FRIDA version two point one is used in this study.
